#--------------------------------------------------------------------
# Line endings
#--------------------------------------------------------------------
# Source code - always LF
*.zig text eol=lf
*.c text eol=lf
*.h text eol=lf
*.cpp text eol=lf
*.m text eol=lf
*.swift text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.glsl text eol=lf
*.blp text eol=lf

# Config/build files - always LF
*.zon text eol=lf
*.nix text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
CMakeLists.txt text eol=lf
*.cmake text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf

# Text data files - always LF (embedded in Zig, parsed with \n split)
*.txt text eol=lf

# Git hooks - extensionless POSIX sh, run by git's own sh even on Windows.
# A CRLF checkout of these turns the shebang into `#!/bin/sh\r` and the hook
# dies with "bad interpreter" - which on a guard means it silently is not there.
scripts/githooks/* text eol=lf

# Windows resource files - preserve as-is (native Windows tooling)
*.rc -text
*.manifest -text

# Binary files
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary

#--------------------------------------------------------------------
# Merge drivers (T957, Stage 0 of docs/design/windows-parity-merge-back-plan.md)
#--------------------------------------------------------------------
# `union` is git's built-in append-both-sides driver: it needs no local config,
# so unlike core.hooksPath it travels with the repo to every clone and every
# seat. It NEVER reports a conflict, which is exactly why the list below is
# short and deliberate: it may only name files where keeping both sides is
# always the right answer. A source file or any structured syntax (.zig, .json,
# .zon, .yml) under a union driver would merge into something that does not
# parse - worse than the conflict it avoided. Acceptance:
# test/win32/upstream-remote.ps1 section D enforces both halves.
#
# .gitignore: ours grew 28 lines and upstream's grew 4, in the same two regions
# of a file where every line is an independent entry. That is a textual conflict
# in every one of the six merge stages, and there is no version of resolving it
# by hand that is better than keeping both sides' entries.
.gitignore merge=union

# The two shared append-only ledgers. Both seats append to these on the same
# branch, so the conflict they produce is not a merge-stage problem - it is a
# `git pull --rebase` on any turn where the other seat pushed first. Each entry
# is a self-contained block, so union keeps both entries whole; the worst case
# is two entries ordered by luck rather than by clock, against a hand
# resolution of a file that is history-only by charter.
docs/design/windows-parity-log.md merge=union
docs/design/windows-parity-feature-ideas.md merge=union

